<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Use translated concept answers, distinguish recodes from column renames, and harmonise two different site vocabularies.
</div>

This chapter uses the 24-person synthetic questionnaire test fixture described in [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
**You need:** the packages and the matching practice connection described in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start).
Run the sections in order in one R session. All printed results below were computed
with dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.5. They are teaching data, not clinical findings.

## Start this chapter

Start a fresh R session in the `book-work` directory created in
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#get-the-book-helpers). Run this setup first:

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/fixture.R")
practice <- book_fixture()
conns <- practice$conns
names(conns)
```

The `site` connection is ready. This chapter creates its temporary questionnaire fixture locally.

## Translation follows the value type

A concept-valued answer is a category, not a quantity to average. In language 2,
the declared value column supplies that type. Your variable name does not need
the suffix `_concept_id`.

```{r category-variable}
education <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900001L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))
class(education)
```

The event selects the education question; the reducer reads its last coded answer.
Selecting the question concept as the value would answer a different question.

```{r default-translation}
categories <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = education),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
print(recipe_execute(categories, out = c(people = "D_categories"), conns = conns))
```

Translation is the default. The data values become labelled factors on the server.

```{r default-table}
ds.omop.table("D_categories", "education", conns = conns)
```

You see category names and released counts. The concept IDs used in the definition
are vocabulary identifiers; they do not appear as numeric answers in this table.

## Recode answers to yes and no

An explicit label map changes categories by concept identity. It does not search
for text in each site's database.

```{r recode}
fever <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900002L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"),
  labels = c("900301" = "yes", "900302" = "no"))
class(fever)
```

Only the declared answer concepts receive those labels. A missing answer stays
missing; absence of a record is not a clinical “no”.

```{r recode-run}
answers <- omop_recipe(variables = list(fever = fever),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
print(recipe_execute(answers, out = c(people = "D_category_answers"), conns = conns))
```

```{r recode-table}
recode_table_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_category_answers$fever", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(recode_table_result))
```

<details><summary>Full aggregate response</summary>

```{r recode-table-full}
recode_table_result
```

</details>

This real ds.table call excludes missing answers and shows the supplied labels.
`ds.omop.table()` additionally retains the complete published category contract,
including declared zero levels, and handles punctuation in labels.

## Rename the column separately

A column name identifies the variable; a label names one of its categories.
Name an explicit variable in the recipe list, without changing what its answers mean.

```{r column-rename}
renamed <- omop_recipe(variables = list(schooling = education),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
print(recipe_execute(renamed, out = c(people = "D_renamed_categories"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_renamed_categories", datasources = conns)
```

The list name supplies the scalar variable name. A concept label map would instead
change values inside that column. The visit walkthrough uses a value-preserving
mutate alias when adding a new name to an already assigned date column.

```{r renamed-table}
ds.omop.table("D_renamed_categories", "schooling", conns = conns)
```

The education categories and counts are unchanged. Next we address differences
between sites, where even the vocabulary names can vary.

## Rename automatically generated concept columns

Some layouts expand one event into separate columns for each concept. A panel
of inpatient/outpatient counts is one example. `column_names` targets their
stable generated keys, not the explicit scalar variable from the previous step.

```{r generated-rename}
visit_event <- omop_event("visit_occurrence", c(9201L, 9202L), name = "visits")
visit_population <- omop_population("visitors", index = omop_index(visit_event))
renamed_panel <- omop_recipe(events = list(visit_event),
  populations = list(visit_population),
  column_names = c(visits_9201_count = "admissions", visits_9202_count = "outpatient"),
  outputs = omop_output("panel", rows = "visitors", format = "person_period",
    options = list(sources = list(visit_event), bins = list(width = 30L),
      window = omop_window(0, 29), analyses = "count")))
recipe_explain(renamed_panel)$selected
```

The keys combine the source name, concept identity and analysis. They remain
stable when a vocabulary changes its wording; your chosen column names remain
readable. An explicit name cannot collide with a protected or another final column.

```{r generated-run}
print(recipe_execute(renamed_panel, out = c(panel = "D_renamed_panel"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_renamed_panel", datasources = conns)
```

The resulting count columns have your declared names. Using `column_names` with
an explicit scalar name such as `education` would not rename that scalar column.

## Open two practice connections

The other lessons use one server. This check uses two DSLite
connections. Both synthetic databases hold the same answer IDs. The second
vocabulary calls University education “University qualification” and declares
a newer vocabulary version. No patient records are downloaded.

```{r two-connections}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
pair <- book_fixture(two_sites = TRUE)
two_conns <- pair$conns
names(two_conns)
```

Each name represents a separate DataSHIELD session with its own private table.

## See the vocabulary difference

```{r two-vocabularies}
lookup <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(900102L, conns = two_conns)
lapply(lookup$per_site, function(x) x["concept_name"])
```

This is permitted vocabulary metadata, not observed answer frequencies.

## Declare a shared label

```{r harmonised}
harmonised <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900001L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"),
  labels = c("900102" = "University education"))),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
print(recipe_execute(harmonised, out = c(people = "D_shared"), conns = two_conns))
```

A common explicit label resolves the known wording difference. Harmonisation is
based on concept identity and the published label contract, not on matching
patient records or guessing that similar words mean the same thing.

## Compare released category sets

```{r site-tables}
site_tables <- lapply(names(two_conns), function(site)
  ds.omop.table("D_shared", "education", conns = two_conns[site]))
names(site_tables) <- names(two_conns)
site_tables
```

```{r identical-categories}
identical(dimnames(site_tables[[1]]), dimnames(site_tables[[2]]))
```

Both real executions returned the same category set. Now combine their counts:

```{r pooled-categories}
ds.omop.table("D_shared", "education", conns = two_conns)
```

The pooled counts add released cells. They do not link people across sites.
In a real network, confirm the concept meanings as well as their labels.

## Try it

Check the selected recipe language once more, then close both temporary connections.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>


```{r solution}
recipe_explain(harmonised, conns = two_conns)$selected
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = two_conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(two_conns)
unlink(pair$path)
```

</details>

## Exercise 2: a common variant

Does a label map change a concept set? Inspect the recipe object locally.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-2}
class(harmonised)
```

A recipe definition is local. Reconciliation changes presentation; concept meaning still requires study review.

</details>

## Exercise 3: a pitfall to avoid

Check that the recipe remains serialisable after harmonisation.

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-3}
is.character(recipe_export_json(harmonised))
```

TRUE means this definition can be recorded. It does not prove that two different concepts are clinically equivalent.

</details>
## Recap

Concept values translate by default. Label maps recode answers; column maps rename variables. Shared labels give common categories across vocabulary wording differences, after you confirm concept meanings.

